.TH BRICK 1
.SH NAME
brick \- pair with, configure and mount a styxbrick
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B brick key
[
.B -d
.I keydir
]
.br
.B brick pair
[
.B -d
.I keydir
]
.I usbmnt
.br
.B brick wifi
.I usbmnt
.br
.B brick mount
[
.B -ab
] [
.B -d
.I keydir
]
.I addr
.I mountpoint
.SH DESCRIPTION
A styxbrick is a microcontroller that serves its hardware
(LED, GPIO pins, ADC, temperature sensor) as a Styx file tree.
On its USB serial port it serves the tree without authentication,
and also a
.B /config
directory; physical access is the trust boundary there.
That port is mounted with
.IR mount (1)'s
.B -A
option, for example
.EX
	mount -A '#U*/dev/ttyACM0' /n/brick
.EE
(after setting the host's tty to raw mode).
On the network the brick serves only inside TLS 1.3,
and only to the client it was paired with.
Each side identifies the other by a pin: the SHA-256 hash
of its certificate's SubjectPublicKeyInfo, in hex.
No certificate authority is involved.
.PP
.B "brick key"
prints our pin, making a key and certificate first if there are none.
.PP
.B "brick pair"
gives the brick mounted over USB at
.I usbmnt
our pin (in
.IB usbmnt /config/peer\fR)
and saves its pin (read from
.IB usbmnt /config/spki\fR)
as
.IB keydir /brick.spki\fR.
Because both pins travel over the USB cable, nothing the network
could alter is trusted.
.PP
.B "brick wifi"
copies standard input, which should be
.EX
	ssid \fIname\fP
	pass \fIpassphrase\fP
.EE
to
.IB usbmnt /config/wifi\fR
in a single write.
The brick never lets the passphrase be read back.
.PP
.B "brick mount"
dials
.I addr
(port 564 if none is given),
makes a TLS 1.3 connection presenting our certificate and accepting
only the server key pinned in
.IB keydir /brick.spki\fR,
and mounts the brick's tree on
.IR mountpoint .
The
.B -a
and
.B -b
options are as for
.IR bind (1).
The connection is closed when the tree is unmounted.
.PP
.I Keydir
defaults to
.BI /usr/ user /keyring/brick
and holds
.TP
.B client.key
our P-256 private key, as 64 hex digits (mode 600)
.TP
.B client.crt
our self-signed X.509 certificate, in DER
.TP
.B brick.spki
the brick's pin
.SH EXAMPLE
Pair over USB, give the brick its network, then use it from anywhere:
.EX
	mount -A '#U*/dev/ttyACM0' /n/brick
	brick pair /n/brick
	brick wifi /n/brick <creds
	cat /n/brick/net/status
	unmount /n/brick
	brick mount 192.168.1.23 /n/brick
	echo 1 >/n/brick/led
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/brick.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mount (1),
.IR bind (1)
